Again $C_6, C_2$ and $C_7$ are in series will give,

\begin{aligned}
& \therefore \quad \frac{1}{C_d}=\frac{1}{C_2}+\frac{1}{C_7}+\frac{1}{C_c} \\
& =\frac{1}{3}+\frac{1}{3}+\frac{1}{3}=\frac{3}{3} \\
& \Rightarrow \quad C_d=1 \mu \mathrm{F} \\
&
\end{aligned}

Again $C_d$ is in parallel with $C_9$, and give,

